					GitHub processes technically necessary connection data, in particular your IP address, date and time, the
					address requested, browser and operating system. According to the GitHub Pages documentation, the visitor's IP
					address is logged and stored for security purposes, whether or not you are signed in to GitHub. The publisher
					has no access to these logs.
				</p>
				<p>
					Legal basis: Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR (legitimate interest in providing the website securely and reliably).
					Retention: GitHub's Pages documentation does not state a fixed period; GitHub keeps the data for the period set
					out in{' '}
					<a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" rel="noopener">GitHub's privacy statement</a>.
				</p>
				<p>
					Transfer outside the EU: the data may be transferred to the USA. GitHub states that it is certified under the
					EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework (including the UK Extension and the Swiss-U.S. Data Privacy Framework); the
					listing is in the{' '}
					<a href="https://www.dataprivacyframework.gov/participant/6174" rel="noopener">Data Privacy Framework list</a>.
					GitHub's documentation also describes Standard Contractual Clauses as a transfer safeguard.
				</p>

				<h2>4. Audience measurement with Umami (self-hosted)</h2>
				<p>
					We measure audience with Umami, which we run ourselves (not Umami Cloud). The measurement script is loaded
					from our own instance at <code>umami-data-events.vercel.app</code> and only counts visits to the domain{' '}
					<code>frenchdataevents.fr</code>. The instance runs on Vercel (Vercel Inc., USA); the measurement data is
					kept in a database at Supabase, in the European Union region. Umami works without cookies.
				</p>
				<p>
					What is recorded: page views and single actions (for example a click on a sign-up link or on "Subscribe to
					calendar") with the page address, referrer, browser, operating system, device type, screen size, language and a
					rough country of origin. No profiles are built.
				</p>
				<p>
					According to Umami's documentation, visitors are grouped through an anonymous session identifier that is a hash
					of the website ID, the visitor's IP address, the browser's User-Agent and a rotating salt. The IP address is
					processed for this and for approximate location, but is not stored as a field in the analytics record. The salt
					rotates monthly by default; we have not configured anything else. The identifier is not a guarantee of
					absolute anonymity. Separately, the hosting providers involved (in particular Vercel) may process IP addresses
					in their own operational and security logs, as Umami's documentation also points out.
				</p>
				<p>
					Legal basis: Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR (legitimate interest in understanding how the site is used, in a
					privacy-friendly way). The measurement stores nothing on your device and reads nothing from it; only if you
					switch it off yourself is a flag set in your browser (see below). Retention: the measurement data is kept only
					as long as it is needed to analyse how the site is used.
				</p>
				<p>
					Objection: add <code>?notrack</code> to the address of any page on this site, for example{' '}
					<code>{`${SITE_URL}/en/?notrack`}</code>. This sets the entry <code>umami.disabled=1</code> in your browser's
